      {
        "h2": "Picking up due work by area",
        "content": "Maintenance routes are not only weekly mowing. Bed maintenance every other week, shrub pruning a few times a year, fall cleanups and spring cleanups all come due at different times. The trick is to slot them onto the day the crew is already in that zone, not as separate trips.\n\nA Waiting List of properties that are due for their next visit makes this easy. Circle an area on the map, pick the properties in that zone that are due for pruning, and schedule them onto the truck that already runs that zone on Thursday. Done well, the pruning crew never makes a special trip to a neighborhood the mowing crew visits every week. The post on [planning the landscape year](/blog/landscaping/landscaping-seasonal-scheduling-software) covers how cleanups fit around the routes."
      },
      {
        "h2": "When routes need to be rebuilt",
        "content": "Routes drift. Customers cancel, new ones sign up in odd places, one crew ends up with a day that runs until dark while another finishes at two. Rebuild your routes at least once a year, ideally in the off-season, and check them mid-season.\n\nSigns a route needs work: a day that regularly runs long, a crew that keeps skipping the last stop, stops that are clearly out of place on the map, and a lot of drive time showing up in the hours report. When you move a customer to a different day, tell them clearly and early, and a reschedule text when their day changes avoids the \"you didn't show up\" call. For how crews and trucks get assigned in the first place, see [scheduling landscape crews](/blog/landscaping/landscaping-crew-scheduling-software)."
      },
      {
        "h2": "What the crew should see on the phone",
        "content": "A good route is useless if the crew is reading it off a printout that was wrong by 9 a.m. The crew app should show the day's stops in order with navigation, the property notes and any special instructions, and let the crew mark each stop done or skip it with a reason. When the office adds a stop mid-day, it shows up on the phone.\n\nIn IndustryBossPro the office builds the day, auto-routing orders it by distance, and the crew app shows the route with property notes, a time clock and done or skip buttons. Customers can also get a \"you're next\" text so they know to put the dog inside. A route the crew trusts is one they will actually follow, and a followed route is the only one that saves money."
      }
